id: k8s.service.loadbalancer
kind: kubernetes
title: LoadBalancer service
aliases: loadbalancer, external load balancer
tags: networking, service, cloud
questions:
  - how does a LoadBalancer service actually get an external IP?
invariant: The cloud controller provisions one external load balancer per Service of this type.
answer_30s: You request an external load balancer, and the cloud controller manager provisions one and wires it to the Service's NodePorts; you get an external IP or hostname.
answer_2m: |
  This is the part people get wrong: the object only expresses intent. A
  controller (cloud provider, MetalLB, kube-vip) has to exist to satisfy it;
  without one, the Service stays Pending forever. Each Service gets its own
  load balancer, which is expensive, and L7 features (paths, hosts, TLS
  termination) are not its job -- LoadBalancer is L4. That is the standard
  segue to Ingress: one L7 entry point in front of many L4 ClusterIP services.
when_to_use:
  - exposing a single L4 service externally
avoid_when:
  - many HTTP services (use one Ingress instead of N load balancers)
commands:
  - kubectl get svc -o wide => is EXTERNAL-IP assigned or still <pending>
  - kubectl describe svc/api => which events explain a Pending external IP
failure_modes:
  - no cloud controller or LB implementation, so the IP never appears
  - assuming the LB health-checks your app path (it usually checks the node's NodePort)
related:
  - k8s.service.nodeport
  - k8s.ingress
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/#loadbalancer
